t want any more problems like  Andrew walking around demanding rights  what's interesting about this story is  that in his quest for Humanity Andrew is  exposed to the coldness and the kind of  in indifference of humanity but he never  lets that deter him he's still undergoes  all of these operations in order to have  more human like organs and to appear  more human and to feel more human and  eventually this all culminates in a  surgery that will allow his positronic  brain to Decay basically rendering him  mortal one interesting thing that I find  about this is that Andrew does all of  this because the human world wouldn't  accept him otherwise so there's a kind  of pettiness on the part of humanity  that is being exposed here because  Andrew does not look human because he  does not behave human because he does  not die then he is not alive he is not  aware it's a very kind of crab in the  bucket mentality it's kind of like  Humanity saying if we can't be immortal  then neither can you this book is really  exploring the nature of being a second  class citizen Andrew is able to make all  of these creations and sell them to  people and make millions people will buy  his art by his Creations but they will  not accept the fact that he is alive  they are unwilling to Grant him the  rights that they are themselves  guaranteed Asimov was big on this  concept of robot rights because from a  strictly scientific point of view the  human brain is merely a biological  machine due to its complexity  Consciousness is generated from Strictly  a scientific point of view there is no  reason that a sufficiently complex  machine couldn't generate that same  conscious experience Asimov understood  that with A continuous progression of  technology that it was quite possible  that Humanity would have to eventually  deal with this in the real world now as  a Star Trek fan I couldn't help but  think about data when reading about  Andrew data in Star Trek the Next  Generation is on a simi